        "itemId": 17592186702796,
        "kind": "question",
        "knowledgePoint": null,
        "markdown": "Determine whether the following system of equations is consistent or inconsistent. If it is consistent, indicate whether it is consistent dependent or consistent independent.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x + y - 2 &=0 \\\\ 2x + 2y &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations is {{field-1}}.",
        "mathAcademyId": null,
        "requiresCalculator": null,
        "solution": "A system of equations is *consistent* if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, it is called *inconsistent*.\n\nConsistent systems, in turn, can be of the following two types:\n\n- When a system of equations has a *single* solution, it is called *consistent independent*.\n- When a system of equations has an *infinite number* of solutions, it is called *consistent dependent*.\n\nWe can summarize these definitions using the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution.\n\nFirst, we can solve the first equation for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x + y - 2 &= 0 \\\\ x &= 2 - y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=2 - y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the second equation to obtain an equation that we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x + 2y &= 3 \\\\ 2\\left(2 - y\\right) + 2y &= 3 \\\\ 4 - 2y + 2y &= 3 \\\\ 4 &= 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a false statement, we conclude that there are no solutions to the system. Therefore, the system is inconsistent.",
        "status": "incorrect",
        "stepId": "17592186682461",
        "title": "Practice",
        "unanswered": false
      },
      "total": 9
